From 83696d41934a4024fed6f05364c854aa0c29dbcd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Un1box <ci@un1box.org>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 18:43:10 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] engine: pool ES2 client vertex fetches, cache ES2 attribute
 state, and elide VBO index rebasing

Bring the GLES 2.0 draw path (gles_es2_draw / gles_es2_bind_attr) to parity
with GLES 1.1 and cut redundant per-draw CPU work across both APIs:

1. Pool interleaved client-array guest fetches in gles_es2_bind_attr through
   gles_draw_fetch, and expand pooled client fetches in both ES1 and ES2 to
   the convex hull of enabled same-stride sibling arrays in the same vertex
   struct so trailing fields of the final vertex (e.g. UV/color after pos)
   do not miss the pool on every draw.
2. Track host generic-attribute enable and pointer state on GLESArray
   (host_normalized + gles_es2_attr_enable) under gles_array_cache so
   unchanged glEnableVertexAttribArray / glVertexAttribPointer / post-draw
   glDisableVertexAttribArray calls are skipped across draws.
3. Skip glDrawElements index rebasing when all enabled arrays reside in host
   VBOs without CPU widening and [0 .. hi] fits inside every bound VBO,
   preserving a constant base pointer across submesh draws; vectorize index
   min/max scanning on AArch64 NEON.
4. Cache GL_FIXED / GL_BYTE float-widened VBO slices keyed by GLESBuffer
   generation (invalidated on glBufferData, glBufferSubData, glUnmapBufferOES,
   and glDeleteBuffers) and widen packed GL_FIXED arrays 4 lanes at a time
   with AArch64 NEON vcvtq_n_f32_s32.
